= Solution
{parent=a}

An $n$-step <self-avoiding walk> on a <graph> $G=(V,E)$ is a sequence $(v_0,v_1,\ldots,v_n)$ of $n+1$ distinct <graph vertices> such that $\{v_{i-1},v_i\}\in E$ for $1\leq i\leq n$. **It has $n$ edges and no repeated vertex**, including its starting <graph vertex>. The $0$-step <self-avoiding walk> consists of its starting <graph vertex> alone.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Represent the <doubly infinite ladder graph> by $\mathbb Z\times\{0,1\}$, with horizontal <edges> between successive columns and a vertical rung at each column. An allowed <self-avoiding walk> is coded by a word in $R,V$. Two successive $V$ steps revisit the preceding <graph vertex> and are forbidden. Conversely, every word without consecutive $V$ steps is a <self-avoiding walk>: horizontal steps strictly increase the column, and a column is visited vertically at most once. This establishes a <bijection>, not merely an upper bound.

For the <directed ladder self-avoiding walk count>, $\sigma_0=1$, $\sigma_1=2$, and for $n\geq2$ a word either ends in $R$, or ends in $RV$. Removing that final block gives
$$
\sigma_n=\sigma_{n-1}+\sigma_{n-2},\qquad\boxed{\sigma_n=F_{n+2}}.
$$
Let $\varphi=(1+\sqrt5)/2$ be the <golden ratio>, and $\psi=(1-\sqrt5)/2$. The <Binet formula> for the <Fibonacci number> yields
$$
\sigma_n=\frac{\varphi^{n+2}-\psi^{n+2}}{\sqrt5}
=\frac{\varphi^{n+2}}{\sqrt5}\left(1-\left(\frac\psi\varphi\right)^{n+2}\right).
$$
Since $|\psi|<\varphi$, taking $n$th roots gives **$\lim_{n\to\infty}\sigma_n^{1/n}=\varphi$**. This use of the golden-ratio symbol is independent of the connection rate in Question 1.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $c_n$ count $n$-step <self-avoiding walks> on the <square lattice> from one fixed <graph vertex>. Splitting a <self-avoiding walk> after $m$ steps and dropping the avoidance constraint on its suffix gives $c_{m+n}\leq c_mc_n$. The <Fekete lemma> therefore gives the <connective constant> $\mu=\lim c_n^{1/n}$. The <planar dual graph> of the <square lattice> is a translated copy of it, so its <connective constant> is also $\mu$.

Put $q=1-p$ and assume $q\mu<1$. Choose $a>\mu$ with $qa<1$. The definition of the <connective constant> provides a finite $K$ such that $c_j\leq Ka^j$ for every $j\geq0$. A simple dual <graph cycle> of length $\ell$ surrounding $0$ has a <graph vertex> in a box of radius $\ell+1$: its diameter is at most $\ell$, and its coordinate ranges straddle the origin. Choose such a <graph vertex> as the starting point, orient the <graph cycle>, and omit its closing <edge>. The remaining <self-avoiding walk> has length $\ell-1$. Consequently the number $N_\ell$ of these <graph cycles> obeys
$$
N_\ell\leq K_0\ell^2 c_{\ell-1}\leq K_1\ell^2a^{\ell-1}
$$
for fixed finite constants. A specified <graph cycle> is open in <dual bond percolation>, equivalently all its crossed primal <edges> are closed, with <probability> $q^\ell$. Hence
$$
\sum_{\ell\geq L}N_\ell q^\ell\longrightarrow0\quad\text{as }L\to\infty.
$$
To make this tail argument valid for every $q\mu<1$, rather than only extremely small $q$, use a <finite modification of Bernoulli percolation>. Condition all primal <edges> within $\Lambda_N$ to be open. This event has positive <probability> for $p>0$. If the resulting <percolation cluster> of $0$ is finite, its outer boundary contains a simple closed dual <graph cycle> surrounding the whole box. Such a <graph cycle> has length tending to infinity with $N$ and crosses no forced-open <edge>. Under the conditioning its remaining <edge> states retain the original independent law, so its <probability> is still $q^\ell$. Choose $N$ so that the above tail is less than $1/2$. The conditional <probability> that $0$ belongs to an <infinite percolation cluster> is then at least $1/2$, and thus $\theta(p)>0$.

This <connective-constant Peierls bound> proves that every $p>1-\mu^{-1}$ is above or at the onset of positive <percolation probability>. Taking the infimum gives
$$
\boxed{p_c\leq1-\mu^{-1}}.
$$
The case $p=1$ is immediate. This proof uses the <Peierls argument>, without assuming the exact <Harris-Kesten theorem>.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write $\mathcal W_n$ for the deterministic <set> of $n$-step <self-avoiding walks> from $0$. Each uses $n$ distinct <edges>, so <independence> gives
$$
\kappa_n=\sum_{w\in\mathcal W_n}\mathbf1_{\{w\text{ open}\}},\qquad\mathbb E_p\kappa_n=c_np^n.
$$
For $n\geq1$, the function $t\mapsto t^{1/n}$ on $[0,\infty)$ is a <concave function>. The <Jensen inequality> gives the <root-moment bound for open self-avoiding walks>
$$
\mathbb E_p(\kappa_n^{1/n})\leq(\mathbb E_p\kappa_n)^{1/n}=p c_n^{1/n}.
$$
Equivalently, the <Lyapunov moment inequality> compares exponents $1/n$ and $1$, both strictly positive. Taking the <limit superior> and the <connective constant> limit proves
$$
\boxed{\limsup_{n\to\infty}\mathbb E_p(\kappa_n^{1/n})\leq p\mu}.
$$
This includes $p=0$ and $n=1$. No <independence> between the different <self-avoiding walk> indicators is asserted or needed.

